in his new book frontier gavin traveled  to ten countries to find most profitable  investment opportunities his number one  choice is nigeria gavin joins us now  from london  gavin why did nigeria win well the the  book project started off with me asking  the top-performing fund managers those  ones that picked the right countries in  emerging markets during the last five to  ten years which country would be the e/m  the emerging markets growth story of the  future and then i traveled to those  countries with the people that picked  them and then i went back to the group  of fund managers to ask them to rank the  choices of their peers and nigeria came  out number one and mostly that's because  of the demographics of the country if  you think back you know rewind sort of  10-15 years to the origins of the BRICS  growth story Brazil Russia India and  China that was all about these being  large population nations that could  sustain a big consumer boom yeah and the  same thing is for Nigeria this is the  country that according to the UN in our  lifetimes will overtake America's  population to be the third biggest  nation on the planet after India and  China so a huge amount of momentum it's  also a very young population there's got  one of the highest proportions of Under  15's and the base that Nigeria is  starting at this is the country that you  know where there's a lot of subsistence  living there's not a lot of disposable  income generally so there's a big uplift  from there now that's the potential  there's also the risks and we know about  the collapse of the oil price and how  that's devastated Nigeria's agree me we  also know about the violence which has  incurred on the elections Boko Haram in  the north having having killed a lot of  people and so it's not devoid of risks  certainly Gavin one of the things that  development Khanna must say when they  look at the BRICS and also at Nigeria is  that one of the most important factors  is governance not just demographics what  reason do we have for hope that Nigeria  is gonna get this right when Brazil  continues to not get it right well I  mean that's a good point and and  corruption is still what a lot of people  will associate Nigeria with it still  ranks on transparency international  scores  the 40 or so worst countries Buhari the  the new leader  you know most countries that have a new  leader the the new leader is untested in  this case the guy is tested he was a  military dictator during the 80s and  that's not a particularly great record  to look at you know there were all sorts  of cumin rice allegations particularly  going on in that time but he is somebody  who's reputed to live a modest life by  the standards of Nigerian politicians  he's somebody who's made corruption his  number one agenda along with fighting  Boko Haram so this has to be seen but it  certainly from the leadership down there  making the right noises right now  Gavin speaking of Boko Haram how strong  is the extremist group in Nigeria right  now does the military have the capacity  and the will to defeat it well you saw  before the election a six-week campaign  to to make the north clean enough for  voting to happen to wipe out Boko Haram  to the extent that people could actually  get to the polling stations and that was  that was in a way a big success you know  people were very skeptical about why the  election was being delayed for six weeks  they didn't believe that you know after  fighting Boko Haram for six years you  know anything substantial could be  achieved in six weeks but we did see  that roll back the interesting thing  that we're seeing with the fight against  Boko Haram is the neighboring countries  coming together so that Boko Haram  doesn't isn't able to race against the  border to escape when there's a military  offensive
 

